        "## Interpretation and limits\n\n"
        "A document OCR model reads the print it was trained on, and nineteenth-century cursive French is not among it: the "
        "frozen model scored a character error rate of 1.345 on the Belfort development test in " + REC_SHORT + " — worse "
        "than silence, because it "
        "invents printed-looking text for strokes it cannot read. A bounded fine-tuning of the last four decoder layers on "
        "600 transcribed lines moved the **character** error rate well past both baselines in that run (0.759, "
        "against 1.000 for predicting nothing and 0.944 for the constant transcript), but **not the word error rate**: "
        "adapted WER 1.017 is still above the empty baseline (1.000) and the constant transcript (0.999), so on whole words "
        "the adapted model does no better than predicting nothing — most words still contain at least one error. Three of "
        "those 140 lines were read exactly, and the 206 MB adapter reloads line-for-line. That is the claim: the "
        "adaptation contract works end to end on a real labelled set, the model learns to read many of the characters of "
        "this hand but not yet whole words, and the numbers are read as micro and macro rates, per measure, against two "
        "non-adapted baselines and the frozen model, with the hypothesis length beside them rather than in isolation.\n\n"
        "This revision's result is the fresh test: 200 lines of the same collection, scored once after a validation-only "
        "choice; the 140-line development test is reported once beside it because development runs had looked at it. The "
        "validation split that picks the epoch and the learning rate is 60 lines, and both rates are corpus edit distances over one crowdsourced transcription — not a benchmark, not a measure of "
        "reading order or layout. **The split is by line image, not by page:** the dataset has no page or writer field, so "
        "the held-out lines can share pages and hands with the training lines, and the held-out rate is a same-collection "
        "estimate that is likely optimistic for a new register or a new hand (the verbatim-repeated transcripts, which "
        "Section 8 removes, change it little). So a result here says the contract works on one council's minutes, not that "
        "the adapted model handles other hands, other languages, other scripts or your scans; and fine-tuning on a narrow "
        "domain can move the model elsewhere — the printed page re-read in Section 9 was still read exactly, one image of "
        "evidence that four decoder layers did not forget clean print, not a measurement of what else they may have moved. "
        "**Pretraining overlap:** the GOT-OCR 2.0 pretraining data are not enumerated, so overlap with the public "
        "Belfort-line release cannot be ruled out (DAT9); the frozen model's rates above 1.0 make memorisation implausible. "
        "The decoder was adapted, not the vision encoder: what the encoder cannot resolve in a 128-px line squashed into a "
        "1024×1024 square stays unread.\n\n"
